      id: "aw-006",
      part: "s1",
      chapter: 6,
      chapterTitle: "Creating the Perfect Buying Environment",
      pages: "35–38",
      pdfPages: "60–63",
      title: "Creating the Perfect Buying Environment: the layout and first paragraphs set the mood for the sale (Axiom 4)",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 6 · Creating the Perfect Buying Environment · Axiom 4 · pp. 35–38 (PDF 60–63)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Your ad layout and the first few paragraphs of your ad must create the buying environment most conducive to the sale of your product or service.", page: "38 (PDF 63)" },
        { text: "You wouldn’t expect to buy an expensive painting from an Army surplus store and you wouldn’t expect to find much of a bargain at Tiffany’s.", page: "37 (PDF 62)" }
      ],
      idea: `Holding attention isn't the only job of the opening paragraphs. They also have to create a **buying environment**.

**The quiz.** You're a salesperson with an expensive product that takes an hour to explain. Where do you meet the prospect?
1. A fancy restaurant near their office at noon.
2. Their boardroom after lunch.
3. Their health club after work, while you both work out.
4. Their home in the evening, while they babysit three kids.
5. Any of the four could be right.

The answer is **5**, because the best place is whichever environment best suits what you're selling. Exercise equipment? The health club might be perfect. Something for parents and kids? The home, with the kids around, might be ideal. The same holds in print.

**The Honolulu gallery.** Resting in Honolulu after a long trip to the Far East, Sugarman saw a space-scene painting in a Waikiki gallery window. JS&A was known for space-age products, so it would suit his office. The gallery looked elegant; he expected high prices. A well-dressed saleswoman remarked on how beautiful the painting was, he agreed, and she took it off the wall and asked him to follow her. In a back room carpeted floor to ceiling, she hung it facing three easy chairs, turned up classical music, dimmed the lights and left two spotlights on the painting. It looked incredible, and he was ready to pay $2,000 on the spot. (He later sponsored the artist, Mark Rickerson, sold his work through JS&A, and owned 50 of his paintings.)

**Build it early.** The environment has to be set in the first part of the ad, at the same time as reading momentum. She first got him into the store, then slowly into the back room. In print, you have total control, and common sense does the rest: selling at a discount, use big price type and busy graphics so it looks like a discount ad; selling something expensive, show class, refinement, confidence and trust (second quote). A salesperson often can't control the room. You always can.

**His own brands.** Battram Galleries, his collectibles company, used an upscale, conservative look and voice, like an expensive gallery. Consumers Hero, a club selling refurbished bargains, looked clearly bargain-oriented. JS&A ads had one uniform, well-organized format that suited space-age electronics; he rarely changed it, and only to fit a particular product better.

**Copy matters most.** You build the environment with graphics and copy, but especially copy: **how you phrase things, which words you choose and the level of integrity you convey**. A store spends thousands on its environment; you can create one in your copy or the look of your website.

**The full sequence.** Attract attention (headline, photos, logo), get the first sentence read because it's so simple and compelling, then the next and the next. While they read, you build the environment the way the gallery drew him into the back room. That's **Axiom 4** (first quote). Creating it comes from experience, specific knowledge of the product and the customer, and understanding the product's nature (Chapter 2). Chapter 7 adds the next layer: getting the reader in harmony with you.`,
      keyPoints: [
        "The opening has two jobs at once: build reading momentum and build a buying environment",
        "Five-locations quiz: the right place is the environment best suited to what you're selling",
        "Honolulu gallery: back room, easy chairs, music, dim lights, spotlights; he almost bought a $2,000 painting on the spot",
        "Match the environment to the offer: discount looks like a discount ad, expensive looks refined and trustworthy",
        "In copy you control the environment through phrasing, word choice and the level of integrity you convey",
        "Axiom 4: layout + first few paragraphs must create the environment most conducive to the sale"
      ],
      why: "For a ZDub client, the environment is the ad creative, the first lines and the landing page together. A nervous beginner, someone with water on the floor at 11 p.m. and a med-spa client each need a different “back room.” When they clash, people leave, no matter how good the offer is.",
