      title: "Resonating with the Reader: get the reader nodding yes (Axiom 5)",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 7 · Resonating with the Reader · Harmony Is the Key · Axiom 5 · pp. 40–44 (PDF 65–69)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Get the reader to say yes and harmonize with your accurate and truthful statements while reading your copy.", page: "44 (PDF 69)" },
        { text: "In short, we want agreement. We want that head to nod in the affirmative. We want harmony.", page: "42 (PDF 67)" }
      ],
      idea: `There are many ways to create harmony between buyer and seller. Two matter most in print: **get the reader saying yes**, and **make statements that are honest and believable**.

**The Buick salesman.** "Nice day, Mr. Jones." Yes. "You keep your car very clean." Yes. "Since you drive a Buick and we sell Buicks, you could probably use a new one?" Yes. "May I show you our latest model with improvements over yours?" Yes. Each statement is true and fairly obvious, and each one gets a nod. Now imagine he'd opened with "Could you use a new Buick?" and heard "No." The sale takes a bad turn and the harmony is gone. In print, that's the moment the reader turns the page.

**The rule.** Get the reader nodding, or at least make truthful statements they know are right. Never let them disagree. The moment they think *no*, *I don't believe that* or *that doesn't apply to me*, you've lost them. As long as they keep saying yes, believing you and staying interested, you're walking together toward the gallery's back room (Chapter 6).

**Food Crunch.** When a seminar student wrote a good ad that didn't sell, Sugarman would suggest changes or rewrite it if he had a better concept. John Sauer wrote an ad for dehydrated survival food right after the 1973 fuel crisis, when there were lines at gas stations with little or no fuel. John built it around insurance (his headline asked whether you had food insurance). Sugarman felt insurance puts people to sleep. His version (both ads are on p. 42, PDF 67) ran under the headline "Food Crunch," with a subheadline tying the fuel shortage most people hadn't been ready for to a coming food crunch. The opening lines were statements any reader would agree with about taking our food supply for granted, followed by the writer's own memory of finding bare supermarket shelves right after the Kennedy assassination: in a crisis, people think first of their own survival.

Why it worked: the headline and subheadline got the first sentence read; the first sentence was short; and the typical reader who could afford the offer nodded at every line, because each one was interesting and true. It also matched what readers were feeling right then.

**Three things the opening of an ad must do:**
1. Get the reader **reading** the copy (without that you have nothing).
2. Create an **environment** where they feel comfortable trading their hard-earned money.
3. Create **harmony**: they agree that what you say is truthful, interesting and informative (second quote).

**Cycle magazine.** To sell Sugarman ad space, *Cycle* sent him a knockoff of his own ad format and copy style (headline: "Easy Reach"). It could have annoyed him, but they knew he'd recognize the effort and respect the creativity. He did, and he bought space. They spoke his language, literally.

**Consumers Hero.** A few sentences into an ad aimed at bargain hunters during runaway inflation, he wrote that consumers were being robbed, that inflation was stealing their buying power and shrinking their dollars. Readers who felt exactly that were in harmony immediately. He used the customer's own words and feelings.

**Axiom 5** (first quote). Chapter 19 develops this as trigger #16 Harmonize, alongside #2 Honesty and #4 Credibility; Chapter 8 shows the full Consumers Hero ad in action.`,
