      idea: `The start of the copy is one of the most important parts of the slide, and Sugarman often opens with a **story** or an offbeat **news item** his readers would enjoy: unusual, always interesting, a good short story.

**Last Wish (the Precor rower).** He took a real news story about a trusted company accountant caught embezzling and built an ad around it, headlined "Last Wish." The trick in the story was real and clever. The accountant wrote company checks to a phony contractor, "Cashin Electric Company." He actually made them out to "Cash," cashed them himself, and once the canceled checks came back from the bank, typed "in Electric Company" after the word "Cash." Eventually his boss noticed big payments to a company that didn't exist. Sugarman gave the prisoner a made-up name and a sympathetic wife who asks that he be allowed an exercise machine for his cell, something compact that gives a full workout. He picks a Precor rower, and the copy moves into why it's so good.

Then comes a **confession**: the embezzlement was real, but the name and the wife's request were invented. Sugarman admits he got a little silly one night trying to find a fresh way to share his enthusiasm for the rowers, and that copywriting sometimes drives you a bit bonkers. The story had nothing to do with rowing, yet it started the slide powerfully, and the confession kept the reader's trust (Axiom 5).

**Related or unrelated.** In *Forbes* he found a short item on a survey of 1,000 British adults: a surprising share of men wore the same underwear for days running, and many women kept wearing theirs after it went gray. He clipped items like that and kept a collection. Two ways he'd use this one:
- **A natural tie.** On QVC in England he met a man selling **Scrub Balls**, nine golf-ball-sized spheres that tumble in the washer and clean clothes with less detergent and water. A print ad could open on the British underwear problem, turn to the reader (how often do you change yours?), reveal that Americans have a waste problem of their own, and bring in the product.
- **An admitted stretch.** The same item could open an ad for a pocket computer: admit the story has nothing to do with the computer except for one important fact you'll reveal later, run through the features while playing off the story, and end on a feature that ties back. The admitted gap is itself a reason to keep reading.

**Save those articles** (both quotes). Selling a rower with a jail story or a computer with underwear statistics may sound far-fetched, but an interesting piece of information blended well with your product gets every word read.

**Magazines do it too.** Some start stories in larger type, which feels less imposing; *Quest/80* began a feature on its front cover and continued it inside. Either way, the copy then has to be good enough that you can't put it down. More slide tools come later: seeds of curiosity (Chapter 10, Appendix B), the copy sequence (Chapter 16) and trigger #26 Storytelling (Chapter 19).`,
