      idea: `Chapters 9 to 13 stretch your thinking. This one starts with a circus elephant.

**The elephant and the peg.** A circus elephant is held by a metal collar on its leg, a small chain and a wooden peg in the ground. A 12,000-pound elephant could yank that peg out in one motion, but it doesn't. As a baby it was held by the same collar, chain and peg, and it pulled every day until the collar cut its leg. Pulling hurt and didn't work, so it stopped. The grown elephant never forgot: whenever it's staked down, it "knows" escape is impossible and painful. That's an **assumed constraint**.

**You have them too.** Everyone has the power to be a great copywriter, but a bad English grade, a writing attempt that went badly or wrong advice from a teacher stays in the subconscious and holds us back. Understand the hurt and the limits you've set yourself, and you can break out.

**The nine-dot puzzle.** Nine dots in a 3×3 square: connect all nine with four straight lines without lifting your pen. Try it before you look up the answer in Appendix A (p. 317, PDF 342). Most people fail because they silently assume the lines must stay inside the square. The solution extends lines beyond it. Often the answer to an important problem lies outside the problem area itself.

**The mailing lists.** For his first direct mailing (the first pocket calculator sold by mail, 50,000 pieces, 10 lists), Sugarman picked eight lists that made sense: engineers, accountants, surveyors, wealthy people at home, buyers from a catalog with similar products. For the last two, his list broker suggested presidents of companies with $20 million or more in sales. Joe thought they'd be too busy to open their own mail and a secretary would toss it. He went along anyway. Those two lists were the best; his eight did poorly.

**Five "you can't" moments from his career:**
1. "You can't sell your airplane for over $190,000." He sold it for $240,000 in 10 days.
2. "You can't sell a $600 pinball game by mail." He sold more than 3,000.
3. "Consumers will rip you off if you take credit-card orders on a toll-free line without a signature." He launched toll-free ordering in the US, with great success and very few problems, before everyone else caught on.
4. "This calculator is the biggest bomb in calculator history at retail." He sold over 30,000 by mail at $59.95.
5. "Who'd buy sunglasses by mail without trying them on, and in only one style?" He sold more than 10 million pairs of the same style.

**Three from history:** DEC founder Ken Olsen said in 1977 that no one would want a computer at home; *Popular Mechanics* predicted in 1949 that future computers might weigh *only* 1.5 tons; and a Yale professor gave Fred Smith's paper on reliable overnight delivery a middling grade because it wasn't feasible. Smith founded FedEx. Appendix A lists seven more.

**The point.** You never really know what will work (second quote). The same goes for marketing problems: don't rule anything out. Lateral thinking (Chapter 1) is roughly the opposite of assumed constraints: step away from the problem, think about unrelated situations, and you'll be surprised how often a solution appears. Remember the elephant and the puzzle, and **Axiom 7** (first quote). Note the discipline in his own stories, though: he didn't just believe, he *tested*. The broker's lists went into a real mailing, and the results decided.`,
